You can get a job without a degree, right?

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:17.400
Like Google, Apple, all these companies coming out and saying, you don't need a degree to

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:19.000
work here anymore,

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:24.920
But I challenge you to walk around the Googleplex or the Apple campus and ask people like,

00:50:24.920 --> 00:50:25.920
hey, do you have a degree?

00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:26.920
Do you have a degree?

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:29.840
You might walk around all day without finding somebody who doesn't have a degree,

00:50:29.840 --> 00:50:33.960
Yes, technically you can get a job at Apple if you don't have a degree,

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:38.160
You can get a job at Google if you don't have a degree, but you probably need to be some

00:50:38.160 --> 00:50:43.840
open source superstar or you need to have like 10 years of sysadmin experience,

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:47.440
There are so many ways that you can get good enough at software development that you could

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:50.700
work at a big tech company without a degree,

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:55.000
But it's rare that somebody just gets hired straight out of high school into such a role

00:50:55.000 --> 00:50:59.800
because getting a degree, first of all, it shows that you have the fortitude to actually

00:50:59.800 --> 00:51:02.000
finish the degree, which is not easy,

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:07.400
And two, it shows that you think long-term and that you've made that investment in yourself,

00:51:07.400 --> 00:51:12.200
And three, it's just like a general way of de-risking people and almost every one of

00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:16.000
these companies, regardless of what they say publicly, they're going to have an HR department

00:51:16.000 --> 00:51:19.440
and they're going to list, maybe they don't put the university degree requirement in their

00:51:19.440 --> 00:51:24.120
job posting, but I guarantee you that the HR people that use filtering software, that's

00:51:24.120 --> 00:51:28.040
going to be one of the factors that they look at when they determine which resume is to

00:51:28.040 --> 00:51:30.120
review and which people to interview,

00:51:30.120 --> 00:51:31.280
It's probably going to be weighted,

00:51:31.280 --> 00:51:34.480
Maybe the degree is only worth like three or four points out of like 20 points that

00:51:34.480 --> 00:51:38.760
they would consider, but it is a consideration,

00:51:38.760 --> 00:51:43.120
And I will tell you, like Free Code Camp, I think we have probably at least five or

00:51:43.120 --> 00:51:48.080
six people on our team of 35 people who do not have a university degree, but we also

00:51:48.080 --> 00:51:50.000
have several people who have PhDs, right?

00:51:50.000 --> 00:51:56.000
So like educational, it depends so much on the individual,

00:51:56.000 --> 00:52:00.320
There are so many considerations, but Free Code Camp is one of the most adventurous institutions

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:05.320
in terms of hiring people who don't have university degrees for coveted remote knowledge worker

00:52:05.320 --> 00:52:06.480
jobs,

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:10.640
In practice, most companies are going to be much more careful about that,

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:16.440
So if you drop out because your company is getting millions of dollars in seed funding

00:52:16.440 --> 00:52:19.960
or you've gotten a whole bunch of clients and you don't feel like you need to go to

00:52:19.960 --> 00:52:21.640
school anymore, that is totally legitimate,

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:26.800
I can totally see why if your career is taking off and college is a distraction, you can

00:52:26.800 --> 00:52:27.800
drop out,

00:52:27.800 --> 00:52:31.160
And in fact, some institutions like Stanford have like a rule that like you can basically

00:52:31.160 --> 00:52:34.120
come back anytime you want and just pick back up your studies,

00:52:34.120 --> 00:52:38.120
Other institutions, if you're gone for a certain number of years, your credits kind of expire

00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:40.080
and you have to start at the beginning, right?

00:52:40.080 --> 00:52:45.080
So you'll want to look at those considerations, but really, unless you're getting some sort

00:52:45.080 --> 00:52:50.280
of crazy product market fit, some sort of crazy traction with your startup, or unless

00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:56.000
some really rich person like offered you a job to like be their kind of personal assistant

00:52:56.000 --> 00:53:01.120
or to build some product for them or something like that, I would not leave college for that,

00:53:01.120 --> 00:53:06.660
Now again, if you study at Stanford or if you study at a university that's more permissive

00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:10.440
and porous as far as coming in and out, you could take a gap year,

00:53:10.440 --> 00:53:14.600
That's totally fine, but I would recommend at some point going back and completing your

00:53:14.600 --> 00:53:20.480
degree even if that's just to go and complete a degree at University of the People or Western

00:53:20.480 --> 00:53:22.480
Governors University,

00:53:22.480 --> 00:53:27.160
So as you can tell, I'm very much in the college degree is important camp,

00:53:27.160 --> 00:53:32.200
You're not going to see me trashing college degree or running Google ads and say like,

00:53:32.200 --> 00:53:33.600
Hey, you don't need a degree,

00:53:33.600 --> 00:53:35.280
Just go to a free code camp,

00:53:35.280 --> 00:53:36.840
I don't believe that at all,

00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:40.480
I believe very much in the benefit of a college education,

00:53:40.480 --> 00:53:42.360
I myself have a college education,

00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:44.040
I even went to a grad school,

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:47.560
Most of my friends went to college,

00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:51.520
Whenever you hear some wealthy person say, Oh, you don't need to go to college anymore,

00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:53.680
Just ask yourself, does that person have a college degree?

00:53:53.680 --> 00:53:59.000
Like, can they really say that objectively because a lot of times people forget all the

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:02.100
things that they learned in college that did help them or they don't see the invisible

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:07.000
benefit of just having HR people not filter you out because you don't have a degree and

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:09.800
getting so many more job interviews and things like that,

00:54:09.800 --> 00:54:14.600
So I believe so strongly that everybody should get a degree and I believe that most people

00:54:14.600 --> 00:54:17.680
who are going to school today should probably get a degree in computer science because it's

00:54:17.680 --> 00:54:18.680
so flexible,

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:20.880
It's the Swiss army knife of tech degrees,

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:24.760
What about free code camps degree program?

00:54:24.760 --> 00:54:30.680
I'm very hardened at the ultra low cost universities that I was talking about earlier, University

00:54:30.680 --> 00:54:35.760
of the people, Western governors university, and how they've dramatically reduced the cost

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:40.760
of being able to get an accredited us university degree,

00:54:40.760 --> 00:54:41.960
I don't think that they've done enough,

00:54:41.960 --> 00:54:45.080
I think education wants to be free,

00:54:45.080 --> 00:54:48.680
It wants for everybody to be able to benefit from it,

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:53.080
And if you really want to make something that everybody can benefit from, you need to make

00:54:53.080 --> 00:54:54.840
it free,

00:54:54.840 --> 00:55:00.640
Think about the 60% of people on earth who live off less than $10 a day,

00:55:00.640 --> 00:55:04.400
Think about all the people who are outside of the United States who are not likely to

00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:07.520
be able to save up enough money to come to the US who are not likely to be able to get

00:55:07.520 --> 00:55:16.280
a US visa, who are living in a rural village in India, who are doing subsistence farming

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:23.360
in a rural China, Chinese town, or living in a favela in Brazil,

00:55:23.360 --> 00:55:28.120
Those people, those families, they're not going to be able to send their kids to learn

00:55:28.120 --> 00:55:29.480
in the United States,

00:55:29.480 --> 00:55:38.960
But you know what they might be able to do?

00:55:38.960 --> 00:55:41.440
They might be able to do free code camp,

00:55:41.440 --> 00:55:46.360
So we're developing a 40-course undergraduate degree,

00:55:46.360 --> 00:55:51.880
So it's an associate's degree in mathematics and a bachelor's degree in computer science,

00:55:51.880 --> 00:55:53.920
And it will take you through everything you need,

00:55:53.920 --> 00:55:54.920
There are no electives,

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:55.920
There are no prerequisites,

00:55:55.920 --> 00:56:00.600
You just start at the top and you progress through these 40 university courses,

00:56:00.600 --> 00:56:02.720
And it's going to be a whole lot of coursework,

00:56:02.720 --> 00:56:05.840
It's going to take several years for most people if they're learning part-time,

00:56:05.840 --> 00:56:08.640
But they'll be able to do it at their own pace for free,

00:56:08.640 --> 00:56:09.960
They'll be able to work through all the labs,

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:12.280
They'll be able to take all the final exams,

00:56:12.280 --> 00:56:17.400
And they'll be able to get a degree from a university that will eventually be accredited,

00:56:17.400 --> 00:56:21.920
It will take until the early 2030s to finish developing all these courses,

00:56:21.920 --> 00:56:26.440
We've already published one of them and we're going to be publishing a lot more soon,

00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:29.840
And then after we publish the entire degree, people have gone through the program, gotten

00:56:29.840 --> 00:56:30.840
the degree,

00:56:30.840 --> 00:56:36.440
It'll still take some time for us to have enough longitudinal data to be able to get

00:56:36.440 --> 00:56:39.800
accredited by one of the big national accreditation agencies,

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:43.040
But that is what we've committed to and we're making steady progress toward it,

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:50.240
I think it's absolutely vital that we teach the correct current skills,

00:56:50.240 --> 00:56:54.720
So you're going to see not only that we teach eight courses of engineering math, which I

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:59.200
think mathematics is incredibly important, especially if you want to get into like machine

00:56:59.200 --> 00:57:00.960
learning,

00:57:00.960 --> 00:57:02.400
We also want to teach security,

00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:03.840
We want to teach ethics,

00:57:03.840 --> 00:57:08.440
We want to teach a whole lot of general ed credit that a university would traditionally

00:57:08.440 --> 00:57:11.760
have, epistemology, topics like that,

00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:15.560
And we want to teach it in a way that you can progress,

00:57:15.560 --> 00:57:19.680
Get an associate degree halfway through and then you can progress if you'd like and get

00:57:19.680 --> 00:57:23.200
the full bachelor's degree at the end,

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:28.120
Our thinking is we want the most middle-of-the-road, conventional university degree we can,

00:57:28.120 --> 00:57:32.960
We've looked at the top 20 computer science degree programs in the United States, MIT,

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:36.560
Caltech, Harvey Mudd, Stanford,

00:57:36.560 --> 00:57:43.080
We've looked at how their universities are configured in terms of the courses that people

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:46.200
take as part of their degree programs,

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:51.840
And we've essentially made a composite degree that hits all those highlights and it's again,

00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:55.200
it's the most middle-of-the-road computer science degree you can imagine if you're just

00:57:55.200 --> 00:58:01.760
looking at top universities that are likely to turn out graduates who are likely to work

00:58:01.760 --> 00:58:11.480
in industry at big tech or potentially get jobs at banks, hospitals, lots of developer

00:58:11.480 --> 00:58:12.480
jobs,

00:58:12.480 --> 00:58:18.040
Most software developer jobs are not in big tech, they're in other ancillary industries,

00:58:18.040 --> 00:58:22.400
And also, of course, many of those people that study those computer science programs

00:58:22.400 --> 00:58:28.760
go on to found startups to get venture capital funding or to be able to bootstrap to success,

00:58:28.760 --> 00:58:33.960
And so we're going to be focused on just giving people the skills that they'll have the optionality

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:39.280
to work freelance, to work in big tech, to work at a smaller regional company or to potentially

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:42.400
build a startup,

00:58:42.400 --> 00:58:45.240
What determines job compensation?

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:46.360
Right,

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:52.600
So compensation, how much money you're making for your time, and that's a way to think of

00:58:52.600 --> 00:58:55.960
getting compensated is essentially you're selling your time to an employer and they're

00:58:55.960 --> 00:58:58.440
paying you for that time,

00:58:58.440 --> 00:59:00.720
It's a buying and selling process, right?

00:59:00.720 --> 00:59:05.200
So of course, first is just labor market economics,

00:59:05.200 --> 00:59:12.360
You have your supply, you have your demand, and then you have your price here in the middle,

00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:19.700
So it depends a lot on what the market is like in terms of the overall economy and things

00:59:19.700 --> 00:59:20.700
like that,

00:59:20.700 --> 00:59:25.200
If there are a lot more people that are looking for jobs than there are jobs available, employers

00:59:25.200 --> 00:59:27.080
can afford to pay less and things like that,

00:59:27.080 --> 00:59:31.560
So just keep in mind economics, right?

00:59:31.560 --> 00:59:32.560
That's one,

00:59:32.560 --> 00:59:35.920
Two, skills,

00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:41.360
So one of the reasons that doctors get paid so much money in the United States is because

00:59:41.360 --> 00:59:44.000
it takes a lot of work to become a doctor,

00:59:44.000 --> 00:59:46.560
After you finish high school, you've got to go to college,

00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:48.400
Then you have to get into medical school,

00:59:48.400 --> 00:59:49.760
That's another four years,

00:59:49.760 --> 00:59:54.440
Then after you finish medical school, you have to do three to five years of residency

00:59:54.440 --> 00:59:57.760
where you work with other physicians and basically are supervised,

00:59:57.760 --> 01:00:03.000
And then you can become fully cool to go start your own practice or work for a big hospital

01:00:03.000 --> 01:00:05.800
chain or whatever you want to do as an independent physician,

01:00:05.800 --> 01:00:07.920
So if you think about that, that's so much additional,

01:00:07.920 --> 01:00:11.520
It's like 12 extra years of education that you need to get before you can do it to build

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:12.520
up those skills,

01:00:12.520 --> 01:00:14.800
So naturally, you're going to want to be compensated more,

01:00:14.800 --> 01:00:17.680
And so physicians tend to be compensated more,

01:00:17.680 --> 01:00:18.680
Software engineering, same way,

01:00:18.680 --> 01:00:22.760
You have to study computer science and you have to build up your skills and prove your

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:23.760
skills,

01:00:23.760 --> 01:00:28.960
But the difference between a developer who just got out and maybe is making like 50,

01:00:28.960 --> 01:00:34.720
60, $70,000 as a software engineer and an engineer that's working on recommendation

01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:40.320
algorithms at Google, I mean, they could just be an engineer without any charges, people

01:00:40.320 --> 01:00:41.320
who are working under them,

01:00:41.320 --> 01:00:45.640
They could be an IC, an individual contributor, and they could be making a million dollars

01:00:45.640 --> 01:00:48.080
a year in compensation as a software engineer, right?

01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:51.760
And it comes down to skills and their ability,

01:00:51.760 --> 01:00:57.240
Three, working conditions,

01:00:57.240 --> 01:01:01.960
So let's think about working in what would traditionally be called a sweatshop,

01:01:01.960 --> 01:01:06.600
Like you might be in circumstances that are literally illegal, doing piecework, having

01:01:06.600 --> 01:01:14.000
a boss who's lording over you, having dangerous machines that you're like a loom or sewing

01:01:14.000 --> 01:01:17.920
machine or something like that that you can get hurt on, right?

01:01:17.920 --> 01:01:24.040
To some extent by getting better working conditions, being able to work in an air conditioned office

01:01:24.040 --> 01:01:29.160
or being able to work at home as a remote worker, like those are kind of forms of compensation,

01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:33.840
And often that, like your compensation will be slightly lower if you have like a posh

01:01:33.840 --> 01:01:37.600
situation where you can be sitting down all day, for example,

01:01:37.600 --> 01:01:40.720
And then four, of course, danger,

01:01:40.720 --> 01:01:46.880
So the most dangerous jobs in the United States are working as a lumberjack, working on cell

01:01:46.880 --> 01:01:50.960
phone towers, where you could potentially fall to your death if there's a mistake,

01:01:50.960 --> 01:01:55.800
There are so many different jobs that you get compensated because they know that you

01:01:55.800 --> 01:01:59.280
could physically get injured or killed doing the job,

01:01:59.280 --> 01:02:03.900
Being a trucker is a very dangerous job, and it's very demanding physically to be sitting

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:04.900
there all day,

01:02:04.900 --> 01:02:07.360
I mean, it's wearing out your back,

01:02:07.360 --> 01:02:11.800
You may have to have a lot of sleepless nights to deliver your load on time,

01:02:11.800 --> 01:02:16.800
So those four things all determine compensation to an extent,

01:02:16.800 --> 01:02:22.080
But the one thing that you had the most control over, you can't really control this at all,

01:02:22.080 --> 01:02:25.680
Working conditions, you just need a certain floor for your working conditions and your

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:27.060
probably good,

01:02:27.060 --> 01:02:32.400
Some people might be willing to work in more hostile conditions like, let's say, going

01:02:32.400 --> 01:02:35.400
and working in a war zone or something like that to increase your compensation,

01:02:35.400 --> 01:02:38.720
Again, that also kind of feeds into danger a little bit,

01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:41.640
But really, what can you control?

01:02:41.640 --> 01:02:42.640
You can control your skills,

01:02:42.640 --> 01:02:47.240
And that's probably the most rational thing to optimize, is trying to get as high skills

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:48.240
as possible,

01:02:48.240 --> 01:02:52.480
And that's why I would encourage people to learn software engineering and programming

01:02:52.480 --> 01:03:00.200
because it's a very remunerative skill set to have,

01:03:00.200 --> 01:03:05.760
So first, I want to say thank you for hearing me out, for listening to my opinions on a

01:03:05.760 --> 01:03:06.760
lot of these different things,

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:08.400
I hope they're relevant to you,

01:03:08.400 --> 01:03:14.360
I want to encourage you to continue developing skills,

01:03:14.360 --> 01:03:18.120
You don't have to go to university to learn these skills,

01:03:18.120 --> 01:03:22.160
You certainly don't have to go back to university and get a master's degree,

01:03:22.160 --> 01:03:23.920
And we didn't really even talk about master's degrees,

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:24.920
I do have a master's degree,

01:03:24.920 --> 01:03:25.920
I have an MBA,

01:03:25.920 --> 01:03:28.560
I have a lot of friends who have master's degrees,

01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:31.080
In general, I'll pull up the chart again,

01:03:31.080 --> 01:03:34.320
In general, you do make more compensation for getting a master's degree,

01:03:34.320 --> 01:03:37.360
But if you look at the difference between having a bachelor's degree, having a master's

01:03:37.360 --> 01:03:41.760
degree, and having a bachelor's degree, and no degree, it's dramatic,

01:03:41.760 --> 01:03:44.160
Like you double your income for getting a master's degree,

01:03:44.160 --> 01:03:49.200
And you may be like get an extra 10%, 15% of income if you get a master's degree, depending

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:51.560
on what you get the master's degree in,

01:03:51.560 --> 01:03:56.240
You may be able to get somebody to pay to send you back to get a master's degree later

01:03:56.240 --> 01:03:57.240
in life,

01:03:57.240 --> 01:04:01.000
Like your employer might do that as a perk and say, oh, we'll pay for you to get an MBA

01:04:01.000 --> 01:04:03.160
or we'll pay for you to get a master's in computer science,

01:04:03.160 --> 01:04:04.360
If you can do that, go for it,

01:04:04.360 --> 01:04:06.520
I think it's great if you're not going to pay for it,

01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:08.920
But I don't think the ROI is necessarily there,

01:04:08.920 --> 01:04:12.520
So I would encourage you to continue to learn your skills through free Code Camp,

01:04:12.520 --> 01:04:16.380
One question that we didn't get into is if you already have a university degree, should

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:18.480
you go back and get a second undergraduate degree?

01:04:18.480 --> 01:04:23.040
And I would say in like 99% of cases, that's not worth it,

01:04:23.040 --> 01:04:25.960
Just learn to code on your own, self-teach,

01:04:25.960 --> 01:04:29.000
There are literally millions of people who are self-teaching,

01:04:29.000 --> 01:04:33.100
There are tens of thousands of people that I know of who I've directly confirmed have

01:04:33.100 --> 01:04:37.720
gotten their first software development job after finishing free Code Camp,

01:04:37.720 --> 01:04:39.840
And many of those people did not have a degree,

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:43.280
Also I'll tell you that if you listen to the free Code Camp podcast, which I host each

01:04:43.280 --> 01:04:47.200
week, just go in your podcast player of choice and search free Code Camp and you'll find

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:51.520
my podcast, I talk to a lot of people who don't have university degrees who are doing

01:04:51.520 --> 01:04:53.640
just fine as software engineers,

01:04:53.640 --> 01:04:57.000
So everything I've said here, there are always exceptions,

01:04:57.000 --> 01:05:02.440
I think in terms of maximizing your likelihood of being able to earn a living, to provide

01:05:02.440 --> 01:05:09.760
for your family, to have a clear forward and upward career trajectory, getting an undergraduate

01:05:09.760 --> 01:05:11.600
degree is the correct thing to do,

01:05:11.600 --> 01:05:19.040
I think getting it as inexpensively as you can, doing in-state tuition, doing community

01:05:19.040 --> 01:05:26.680
college clapping and AP crediting out of certain courses or doing an ultra low cost for your

01:05:26.680 --> 01:05:27.680
university,

01:05:27.680 --> 01:05:32.000
I think anything you can do to reduce your cost is by definition going to increase your

01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:37.560
ROI and any money you can save today and invest is going to compound over time,

